NCT02413320_33208340,Number of Participants With Pathological Complete Response [Time Frame: 20 weeks],"One hundred patients were randomized; arm A (n = 48) or arm B (n = 52). pCR was 54% [95% confidence interval (CI), 40%-69%] in arm A and 54% (95% CI, 40%-68%) in arm B. RCB 0+I rate was 67% in both arms. Median sTIL density was numerically higher in those with pCR compared with those with residual disease (20% vs. 5%; P = 0.25). At median follow-up of 38 months, EFS and OS were similar in the two arms. Grade 3/4 adverse events were more common in arm A compared with arm B, with the most notable differences in neutropenia (60% vs. 8%; P < 0.001) and febrile neutropenia (19% vs. 0%; P < 0.001). There was one treatment-related death (arm A) due to acute leukemia. Mean treatment cost was lower for arm B compared with arm A (P = 0.02).",0.5084,49.16,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The published primary endpoint is pCR, which is the same clinical construct as the currently registered pathological complete response endpoint. The publication does not restate the registered 20-week timeframe, but no different timeframe is affirmatively reported; this is missing detail rather than evidence of modification. Additional outcomes are reported, but the registered primary endpoint remains intact. The original prospective registry version and change history are unavailable. The current registry entry was therefore treated cautiously and was not used to infer a major switch. The current registered primary, pathological complete response at 20 weeks, is clinically equivalent to the published pCR endpoint.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.88,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Pathological complete response (pCR), reported as 54% in arm A and 54% in arm B",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Participants With Pathological Complete Response at 20 weeks"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Pathological complete response (pCR)"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_t