NCT01036087_29879283,Number of Participants That Achieved Pathologic Complete Response (CR) [Time Frame: Assessed after 14 weeks (following PNC and FEC preoperative chemotherapy treatment).],"Forty-seven patients were accrued; 7 were ineligible. The 40 enrolled women had a median age of 57 (range, 23-68) years; 29 (72%) were postmenopausal. Three patients did not complete therapy because of toxic effects (n = 2) or distant metastasis (n = 1). Nineteen patients had triple-negative and 21 had hormone receptor-positive IBC. The pCR and pCR rates were overall, 11 of 40 (28%; 95% CI, 15%-44%); triple-negative IBC, 8 of 19 (42%; 95% CI, 20%-66%); and hormone receptor-positive/HER2-negative IBC, 3 of 21 (14%; 95% CI, 3%-36%). During treatment with panitumumab, nab-paclitaxel, and carboplatin, 10 patients were hospitalized for treatment-related toxic effects, including 5 with neutropenia-related events. There were no treatment-related deaths. The most frequent nonhematologic adverse event was skin rash. Several potential predictors of pCR were identified, including pEGFR expression and COX-2 expression.",0.642,35.8,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary and published primary are both pathologic complete response. The publication reports the registered endpoint directly, although the abstracted text does not restate the registered 14-week assessment timeframe or all registry qualifiers. This is insufficient evidence of a changed endpoint. Treatment-related adverse events are registered as secondary and are also reported, so no primary outcome switch is identified. Only the current registry entry was available, so the original prospective primary outcome cannot be independently confirmed. The current registered endpoint is pCR, and the publication reports pCR as its principal efficacy outcome. No later registry amendment can be assessed.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.96,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Pathologic complete response (pCR), reported o