NCT03839823_38771995,Progression-free Survival [Time Frame: Up to approximately 34 months],"Among 222 patients randomly assigned to ribociclib plus ET (n = 112) or combination CT (n = 110), 150 (67.6%) had symptomatic visceral metastases, 41 (18.5%) had rapid disease progression per investigator's judgment, and 31 (14.0%) had symptomatic nonvisceral disease. Overall, 106 (47.7%) patients had investigator-assessed visceral crisis. The median follow-up time was 37.0 months. At data cutoff, 31.3% (ribociclib arm) and 15.5% (CT arm) of patients had completed study treatment and transitioned to post-trial access. The median PFS was 21.8 months (ribociclib plus ET; [95% CI, 17.4 to 26.7]) and 12.8 months (combination CT; [95% CI, 10.1 to 18.4); hazard ratio, 0.61 [95% CI, 0.43 to 0.87]; P = .003. The overall response rates and the median time to response in the ribociclib versus CT arms, respectively, were 66.1% and 61.8% and 4.9 months and 3.2 months (hazard ratio, 0.76 [95% CI, 0.55 to 1.06]). Lower rates of symptomatic adverse events were observed in the ribociclib versus CT arm.",0.4615,53.85,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary and published primary are the same clinical construct: progression-free survival. The publication reports the registered endpoint directly, although it presents median PFS and a follow-up duration of 37 months. No affirmative evidence demonstrates a changed endpoint definition, timeframe, population, or measurement method. Other reported outcomes are secondary analyses and do not constitute a primary outcome switch. Several registered secondary outcomes are not reported in the supplied publication text, but their absence does not indicate primary outcome switching. Registry change history was unavailable, so the current registry entry was used cautiously. The original prospective primary outcome cannot be independently verified; the current registered primary is PFS. The lack of registry history does not support assigning a swit