NCT02600923_37594640,Number of Participants With All-causality Treatment-emergent Adverse Events (TEAEs) [Time Frame: 3 years] | Number of Participants With Palbociclib-related TEAEs [Time Frame: 3 years] | Number of Participants With Serious Adverse Events (SAEs) [Time Frame: 3 years],"A total of 130 patients were treated with palbociclib plus letrozole (Argentina, n = 33; Brazil, n = 35; Colombia, n = 28; Mexico, n = 34). The most common treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) of any grade were neutropenia (70.0%), leukopenia (34.6%), anemia (33.8%), decreased neutrophil count (27.7%), and thrombocytopenia (24.6%); 22.3% of patients required a palbociclib dose reduction due to adverse events (AEs). Serious AEs were reported in 32 patients (24.6%). The ORR was 24.8% (95% confidence interval 17.6‒33.2), and the median duration of treatment was 10.6 months (range 0.1‒29.3).",0.7152,28.48,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The publication reports the same safety/tolerability construct as the currently registered primary outcomes, including TEAEs and SAEs. The publication also reports ORR, but ORR was a registered secondary outcome and therefore is not an outcome switch. The 3-year timeframe and the palbociclib-related qualifier are not fully restated in the supplied publication text, which is insufficient evidence of a modification. The original prospective registry version is unavailable. The original prospective registry version is unavailable and no registry change history can be assessed. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously. Its safety-focused primary outcomes correspond to the publication's reported safety and tolerability outcomes; no major switch is assigned based on the later wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.86,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Safety and tolerability, including treatment-emergent adverse events and serious adverse events.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Participants With All-causality