NCT01982448_32798689,"Number of Participants With Pathologic Response by HR-deficiency (HRD) Status [Time Frame: Evaluated after definitive breast surgery, up to 4-5 months from enrollment.]","One hundred and thirty-nine patients were evaluable for response, including 88 (63.3%) who had surgery at 12 weeks and 51 (36.7%) who crossed over to an alternative provider-selected preoperative chemotherapy regimen due to inadequate clinical response. HRD results were available for 104 tumors (74.8%) and 74 (71.1%) were HRD positive. The RCB-0/1 rate was 26.4% with cisplatin and 22.3% with paclitaxel. No significant association was observed between HRD score and RCB response to either cisplatin [odds ratio (OR) for RCB-0/1 if HRD positive 2.22 (95% CI: 0.39-23.68)] or paclitaxel [OR for RCB-0/1 if HRD positive 0.90 (95% CI: 0.19-4.95)]. There was no evidence of an interaction between HRD and pathologic response to chemotherapy.",0.6737,32.63,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary and published primary address the same clinical construct: pathologic response assessed in relation to HRD status. RCB-0/1 is a pathologic response outcome, and reporting rates, odds ratios, treatment arms, or HRD associations does not create a different endpoint. The publication does not affirmatively demonstrate a changed endpoint definition, timeframe, population, or measurement method. The prespecified secondary outcomes are not reported in the supplied publication text, but missing secondary outcomes do not establish primary outcome switching. The original prospective registry version is unavailable and no registry change history can be retrieved. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously. Because the current wording cannot be shown to predate results, it is not sufficient to infer a switch or modification.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.86,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Pathologic response, defined/reported as RCB-0/1 rate, evaluated by HRD status an