NCT02536339_33945296,"Percentage of Participants With Objective Response in the CNS, Assessed Using Response Assessment in Neuro-Oncology-Brain Metastases (RANO-BM) Criteria [Time Frame: From Baseline until disease progression (assessed every 6 weeks for first 2 scans, followed by every 8 weeks for 2 scans, then every 12 weeks until disease progression; up to approximately 3.5 years)]","Thirty-nine patients were treated for a median (range) of 4.5 (0.3-37.3) months at clinical cutoff. Thirty-seven patients discontinued treatment, most commonly because of CNS progression (n = 27); two remained on treatment. CNS ORR was 11% (95% CI, 3 to 25), with four partial responses (median duration of response, 4.6 months). Clinical benefit rate at 4 months and 6 months was 68% and 51%, respectively. Two patients permanently discontinued study treatment because of adverse events (left ventricular dysfunction [treatment-related] and seizure, both grade 3). No grade 5 adverse events were reported. No new safety signals emerged with either agent.",0.6421,35.79,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary and published primary both measure objective response in the CNS. The publication's use of the abbreviation CNS ORR and its reporting of partial responses do not indicate a different endpoint. The registered assessment schedule and full RANO-BM wording are not restated in the provided publication text, but this is missing detail rather than evidence of modification. Reported clinical benefit and duration of response are registered secondary outcomes, not replacement primary outcomes. Registry change history was unavailable. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously; the absence of the original prospective version prevents independent confirmation of whether the current primary endpoint was amended, but the current wording alone does not support a major switch.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"CNS objective response rate (CNS ORR), reported as 11% with four partial responses.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of Participants With Objective Response in the CNS, assessed using RANO-BM criteria from baseline until disease progression."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""CNS objective response rate (CNS ORR), reported as 11% with four partial responses."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:46:54.079679+00:00
NCT01705691_26126970,Pathologic Complete Response Rate (ypCR) Following Neoadjuvant Therapy in Breast and Axillary Lymph Nodes [Time Frame: At the time of surgery approximately 24 to 28 weeks.],"Locally advanced breast cancer (LABC) is a good setting in which to monitor response to neoadjuvant chemotherapy, to downsize the tumor (which facilitates breast-conserving surgery), and to test newer agents in untreated patients. Eribulin (E) has shown activity in patients who have undergone previous taxane, anthracycline, and capecitabine treatment. We aimed to evaluate the neoadjuvant use of E followed by doxorubicin and cyclophosphamide (AC) in patients with HER2-negative LABC, using as a control a randomized group of women who received weekly paclitaxel (WP). Fifty women with LABC were accrued January-August 2013. Patients were randomized (1:2) to receive either WP (N = 19) for 12 treatments or E (N = 31) every 3 weeks for 4 cycles followed by AC every 3 weeks for 4 cycles before surgery. 17/19 patients who took WP and 25/30 who took E completed all cycles. Patients were evaluated by clinical examination and breast MRI at baseline and after completion of E or WP. Surgical pCR in breast and lymph nodes was determined by a local pathologist following chemotherapy. Forty-nine patients received ≥1 dose of neoadjuvant chemotherapy and are included in this analysis. Forty-eight underwent surgery; one had disease that was inoperable (on E) and is included as no-pCR patient. 17/19 of these patients who took WP completed 12 doses; 28/30 on E completed 4 cycles. Six discontinued treatment on WP, E, or AC. Both treatments were well tolerated. pCR on WP = 5/19(26 %) and on E = 5/30(17 %). Both regimens were equally well tolerated with no unexpected toxicities. pCR did not suggest higher activity with E than with other standard regimens in these LABC patients.",0.6018,39.82,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary endpoint and published primary endpoint are the same clinical construct: surgical/pathologic complete response in the breast and lymph nodes. The publication reports the registered endpoint, although it does not restate the registry timeframe or all operational details. Those omissions do not establish a modification. Secondary outcomes are largely not reported, but their absence does not constitute primary outcome switching. No registry change history is available. The current registry primary outcome was used cautiously; the original prospective outcome cannot be independently confirmed.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Surgical pathologic complete response (pCR) in the breast and lymph nodes, reported by treatment group.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Pathologic Complete Response Rate (ypCR) in breast and axillary lymph nodes at surgery approximately 24 to 28 weeks after initiation of therapy"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Surgical pCR in breast and lymph nodes determined by a local pathologist following neoadjuvant chemotherapy"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:47:06.207617+00:00
NCT03853707_37023705,Progression-free Survival (PFS) [Time Frame: Up to 36 months],"RP2D for Arm A (n = 10) was ipatasertib 300 mg daily, carboplatin AUC2, and paclitaxel 80 mg m-2 days 1, 8, and 15 every 28 days. RP2D for Arm B (n = 12) was ipatasertib 400 mg daily and carboplatin AUC2 days 1, 8, and 15 every 28 days. RP2D for Arm C (n = 6) was likely ipatasertib 300 mg 21 days on 7 days off, capecitabine 750 mg m-2, twice a day, 7 days on 7 days off, and atezolizumab 840 mg days 1 and 15 every 28 days. The most common (≥10%) grade 3-4 AEs at RP2D for Arm A (N = 7 at RP2D) were neutropenia (29%), diarrhea (14%), oral mucositis (14%), and neuropathy (14%); Arm B had diarrhea (17%) and lymphopenia (25%); and Arm C had anemia, fatigue, cognitive disturbance, and maculopapular rash (17% each). Overall responses at RP2D were 29% Arm A, 25% Arm B, and 33% Arm C. PFS was 4.8, 3.9, and 8.2 months for patients on Arms A, B, and C, respectively.",0.406,59.4,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"PFS is the current registered primary outcome and is reported in the publication. Although the paper prominently presents RP2D and safety results, the provided text does not affirmatively establish that these replaced or formally demoted PFS as the published primary endpoint. The apparent discrepancy is therefore treated as insufficient evidence of outcome switching, particularly because the original registry version is unavailable. Only the current registry entry is available, so the original prospective primary outcome and any amendments cannot be established. The current registered PFS outcome was used cautiously. The publication does not explicitly identify a different primary endpoint or disclose a formal endpoint change.",medium,True,True,False,False,,0.58,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Not explicitly stated; the publication prominently reports recommended phase 2 dose (RP2D) and safety/tolerability, while also reporting PFS",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Progression-free Survival (PFS)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""PFS, reported as 4.8, 3.9, and 8.2 months for Arms A, B, and C"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": true, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:47:22.755219+00:00
NCT01864746_33793299,Invasive Disease Free Survival (iDFS) for Palbociclib vs. Placebo in Patients With High CPS-EG Score After Neoadjuvant Chemotherapy Receiving Standard Adjuvant Endocrine Therapy for HR-positive/HER2-normal Primary Breast Cancer. [Time Frame: From date of randomisation to data cut off: 24 August 2020 (approximately 6 years and 6 months)],"One thousand two hundred fifty patients were randomly assigned. The median age was 49.0 years (range, 19-79), and the majority were ypN+ with Ki-67 ≤ 15%; 59.4% of patients had a clinical pathological staging-estrogen receptor grading score ≥ 3. 50.1% received aromatase inhibitor, and 33% of premenopausal women received a luteinizing hormone releasing hormone analog in addition to either tamoxifen or an aromatase inhibitor. After a median follow-up of 42.8 months (92% complete), 308 events were confirmed. Palbociclib did not improve iDFS versus placebo added to ET-stratified hazard ratio, 0.93 (95% repeated CI, 0.74 to 1.17) and two-sided weighted log-rank test (Cui, Hung, and Wang) P = .525. There was no difference among the subgroups. Most common related serious adverse events were infections and vascular disorders in 113 (9.1%) patients with no difference between the treatment arms. Eight fatal serious adverse events (two palbociclib and six placebo) were reported.",0.7034,29.66,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary endpoint and published primary endpoint are both iDFS in the same clinical setting and treatment comparison. The publication directly reports iDFS, including its hazard ratio and conclusion. No affirmative evidence demonstrates a different endpoint, definition, population, timeframe, or measurement method. The absence of the registered secondary outcomes from the provided text does not constitute primary outcome switching. The original prospective registry version is unavailable and no change history can be assessed. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously; its later