NCT01940497_30396014,Percentage of Participants With Treatment-Emergent Adverse Events (TEAEs) [Time Frame: Day 1 up to 28 days after last dose of trastuzumab (up to approximately 1 year)],"Two hundred forty patients were enrolled (adjuvant therapy: 81.7%; neoadjuvant therapy: 18.3%), and 201 completed the treatment (early discontinuation was mainly due to intercurrent adverse events [AEs], 7.5%). Overall, the two cohorts displayed similar safety profiles. From H SC start, the rate of treatment-related AEs in the safety population (N = 228) was 3.9% for grade ≥3 AEs; 0.9% for serious AEs (one pleuropericarditis and one anaphylactic shock, both resolved) and 14.5% for cardiac AEs, the most common being the decreased left ventricular ejection fraction (7.9%; mean reduction from the screening to the follow-up visit was 2.9%). No cases of congestive heart failure occurred. The rate of systemic administration-related reactions and local injection site reactions was 68.0% and 21.9%, respectively, mostly of grade 1-2.",0.5753,42.47,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,minor_modification,"The publication addresses the same broad clinical construct of treatment safety and adverse events, so this is not a replacement by a different endpoint. However, the reported principal results are explicitly framed as treatment-related adverse events rather than the broader registered treatment-emergent adverse-event endpoint. Because the original registry version is unavailable and the publication does not clearly describe this as a protocol amendment, the discrepancy is conservatively classified as an outcome modification rather than a major outcome switch. No registry change history was retrievable. The current registry primary outcome was therefore used cautiously as a proxy for the prospective outcome; the original registered outcome cannot be confirmed, and no major switch is assigned on the basis of the current wording alone.",medium,False,True,False,False,definition_or_timeframe_changed,0.72,Outcome 