NCT01074970_26957554,Two-year Disease Free Survival [Time Frame: 24 months],"In a trial of neoadjuvant platinum, gemcitabine, and iniparib, HR deficiency predicted residual cancer burden score of 0 or I (RCB 0/I) and pathologic complete response (pCR; OR = 4.96, P = 0.0036; OR = 6.52, P = 0.0058). HR deficiency remained a significant predictor of RCB 0/I when adjusted for clinical variables (OR = 5.86, P = 0.012). In two other trials of neoadjuvant cisplatin therapy, HR deficiency predicted RCB 0/I and pCR (OR = 10.18, P = 0.0011; OR = 17.00, P = 0.0066). In a multivariable model of RCB 0/I, HR deficiency retained significance when clinical variables were included (OR = 12.08, P = 0.0017). When restricted to BRCA1/2 nonmutated tumors, response was higher in patients with high HRD scores: RCB 0/I P = 0.062, pCR P = 0.063 in the neoadjuvant platinum, gemcitabine, and iniparib trial; RCB 0/I P = 0.0039, pCR P = 0.018 in the neoadjuvant cisplatin trials.",0.3509,64.91,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,moderate_switch,"Disease-free survival and pathologic response (RCB 0/I or pCR) are different clinical constructs. The publication presents pathologic response outcomes rather than the currently registered two-year disease-free survival outcome. However, the original prospective registry version and amendment history are unavailable, so the evidence supports a moderate classification rather than a major undisclosed switch. Only the current registry entry is available, and the original prospective primary outcome cannot be established. The current two-year disease-free survival entry was therefore used cautiously. Because registry chronology and any amendment timing are unavailable, a major undisclosed switch cannot be assigned solely from the later registry wording. Guardrail applied: Endpoint 1: omission is evidenced but disclosure is unclear; major switch was not assigned.",medium,False,True,False,True,primary_replaced | prespecified_omitted,0.65,Outcome switch — moderate / partly disclo