NCT02278120_29804902,"Progression Free Survival (PFS) by Investigator Assessment [Time Frame: From randomization to first documented progression or death, assessed up to approximately 29 months]","Between Dec 17, 2014, and Aug 1, 2016, 672 patients were randomly assigned: 335 to the ribociclib group and 337 to the placebo group. Per investigator's assessment, median progression-free survival was 23·8 months (95% CI 19·2-not reached) in the ribociclib group compared with 13·0 months (11·0-16·4) in the placebo group (hazard ratio 0·55, 95% CI 0·44-0·69; p<0·0001). Grade 3 or 4 adverse events reported in more than 10% of patients in either group were neutropenia (203 [61%] of 335 patients in the ribociclib group and 12 [4%] of 337 in the placebo group) and leucopenia (48 [14%] and four [1%]). Serious adverse events occurred in 60 (18%) of 335 patients in the ribociclib group and 39 (12%) of 337 in the placebo group, of which 15 (4%) and six (2%), respectively, were attributed to the study regimen. 12 (4%) of 335 patients in the ribociclib group and ten (3%) of 337 in the placebo group discontinued treatment because of adverse events. No treatment-related deaths occurred. 11 deaths occurred (five [1%] in the ribociclib group and six [2%] in the placebo group) during or within 30 days after treatment, most of which were due to progression of the underlying breast cancer (three [1%] and six [2%]). The remaining two deaths in the ribociclib group were due to an intracranial haemorrhage in an anticoagulated patient, and a pre-existing wound haemorrhage in another patient.",0.4675,53.25,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary and published primary are both investigator-assessed progression-free survival. Reporting median PFS and a hazard ratio does not change the endpoint. The publication does not affirmatively report a different timeframe, definition, population, or measurement method. The original registry version is unavailable, so the current entry is used cautiously; this limitation does not provide evidence of outcome switching. The original prospective registry version is unavailable and no registry change history can be assessed. The current registry entry was used cautiously. The publication reports investigator-assessed PFS, which is clinically equivalent to the current registered primary outcome; no major switch is inferred from the unavailable original version or from wording differences.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Investigator-assessed progression-free survival (PFS), reported as median PFS comparing ribociclib plus endocrine therapy with placebo plus endocrine therapy",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Progression Free Survival (PFS) by Investigator Assessment [from randomization to first documented progression or death, assessed up to approximately 29 months]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Per investigator's assessment, median progression-free survival"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": true, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-06T03:11:30.829232+00:00
NCT01097642_25218708,Complete Response Rate [Time Frame: one year after treatment],"Of 79 randomized patients, 77 were treated. Based on an intent-to-treat analysis, an objective response rate of 30% (95% confidence interval [CI], 16.6-46.5) was observed in the monotherapy arm, and 35.9% (95% CI, 21.2-52.8) in the combination arm. Median progression-free survival was 4.1 months in both treatment groups. Safety findings were consistent with the known individual toxicity profiles of ixabepilone and cetuximab. Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders were more common with combination therapy, as were discontinuations because of adverse events.",0.4413,55.87,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,moderate_switch,"Complete response rate and objective response rate are different clinical endpoints, and the publication reports ORR rather than the registered CR rate. Because the original prospective registry version and amendment history are unavailable, a major undisclosed switch cannot be assigned solely from the current registry wording. The discrepancy is therefore classified conservatively as a moderate/partly disclosed outcome switch, with human review required. No registry change history or original prospective primary outcome is available. The current complete response rate entry must therefore be treated cautiously; the evidence supports a discrepancy between the current registered primary and the published primary, but does not establish whether the discrepancy reflects a prospectively documented amendment. Guardrail applied: Endpoint 1: omission is evidenced but disclosure is unclear; major switch was not assigned.",medium,False,True,False,True,primary_replaced | prespecified_omitted,0.65,Outcome switch — moderate / partly disclosed,different,"Objective response rate (ORR), reported by treatment arm","The current registry identifies complete response rate as the primary outcome, whereas the publication reports objective response rate of 30.0% versus 35.9% as the principal efficacy result. | Objective response rate includes responses beyond complete response and therefore is not the same endpoint as complete response rate.",False,Endpoint 1: omission is evidenced but disclosure is unclear; major switch was not assigned.,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Complete Response Rate at one year after treatment"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Objective response rate (ORR), defined/reported as the percentage of patients with objective response"", ""same_construct"": false, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": false, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [""The current registry identifies complete response rate as the primary outcome, whereas the publication reports objective response rate of 30.0% versus 35.9% as the principal efficacy result."", ""Objective response rate includes responses beyond complete response and therefore is not the same endpoint as complete response rate.""], ""classification_label"": ""Outcome switch — moderate / partly disclosed""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,moderate_switch,False,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-06T03:11:44.551184+00:00
NCT02102490_28533223,Percentage of Participants With Complete Response (CR) or Partial Response (PR) (Objective Response Rate [ORR]) [Time Frame: From Date of First Dose until Disease Progression or Death Due to Any Cause (Up To 14 Months)],"Purpose: The phase II MONARCH 1 study was designed to evaluate the single-agent activity and adverse event (AE) profile of abemaciclib, a selective inhibitor of CDK4 and CDK6, in women with refractory hormone receptor-positive (HR+), HER2- metastatic breast cancer (MBC).Experimental Design: MONARCH 1 was a phase II single-arm open-label study. Women with HR+/HER2- MBC who had progressed on or after prior endocrine therapy and had 1 or 2 chemotherapy regimens in the metastatic setting were eligible. Abemaciclib 200 mg was administered orally on a continuous schedule every 12 hours until disease progression or unacceptable toxicity. The primary objective of MONARCH 1 was investigator-assessed objective response rate (ORR). Other endpoints included clinical benefit rate, progression-free survival (PFS), and overall survival (OS).Results: Patients (n = 132) had a median of 3 (range, 1-8) lines of prior systemic therapy in the metastatic setting, 90.2% had visceral disease, and 50.8% had ≥3 metastatic sites. At the 12-month final analysis, the primary objective of confirmed objective response rate was 19.7% (95% CI, 13.3-27.5; 15% not excluded); clinical benefit rate (CR+PR+SD≥6 months) was 42.4%, median progression-free survival was 6.0 months, and median overall survival was 17.7 months. The most common treatment-emergent AEs of any grade were diarrhea, fatigue, and nausea; discontinuations due to AEs were infrequent (7.6%).Conclusions: In this poor-prognosis, heavily pretreated population with refractory HR+/HER2- metastatic breast cancer, continuous dosing of single-agent abemaciciclib was well tolerated and exhibited promising clinical activity. Clin Cancer Res; 23(17); 5218-24. ©2017 AACR.",0.4778,52.22,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,minor_modification,"The registered and published primary outcomes address the same clinical construct, objective response rate. However, the publication explicitly reports the primary ORR at a 12-month final analysis, compared with the registered timeframe of up to 14 months. This supports a timeframe-related outcome modification, not an outcome switch, because the registered ORR was still reported as the primary endpoint. No registry change history was available. The current registry entry was used cautiously as the comparator, while the original prospective primary outcome remains unavailable. The later wording alone does not support a major outcome switch.",high,False,True,False,False,definition_or_timeframe_changed,0.82,Outcome modification (timeframe/definition/population),modified,"Investigator-assessed confirmed objective response rate (ORR), reported at the 12-month final analysis.",The registry specifies ORR through up to 14 months of follow-up. | The publication explicitly reports the primary confirmed ORR at the 12-month final analysis.,False,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Objective Response Rate (ORR): percentage of participants with CR or PR, assessed from first dose until disease progression or death due to any cause, up to 14 months."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Investigator-assessed confirmed ORR, reported at the 12-month final analysis."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": false, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [""The registry specifies ORR through up to 14 months of follow-up."", ""The publication explicitly reports the primary confirmed ORR at the 12-month final analysis.""], ""classification_label"": ""Outcome modification (timeframe/definition/population)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,minor_modifica